00:00:02,170 --> 00:00:04,830
Hi, everybody. My name
is Dr. Joseph Holloway,

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:06,650
and in this session,
we're going to be covering

3
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:10,290
rheumatic fever, vasculitis,
and cardiomyopathy,

4
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:13,290
which are three different
conditions that are

5
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:16,910
brought about by separate
and unique microbes.

6
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:23,590
Now, the specific
diseases that we're going

7
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:26,770
to be discussing today
are rheumatic fever,

8
00:00:26,970 --> 00:00:30,290
Rocky Mountain Spotted
Fever, and Chagas disease.

9
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:32,930
So you're going to want
to be able to understand

10
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:35,570
the basic epidemiology
associated with

11
00:00:35,570 --> 00:00:39,070
each one of those,
along with the etiology.

12
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:42,630
So being able to identify
and describe the microbial

13
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:45,810
pathogen that causes each
one of those conditions.

14
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:48,530
You're going to want to
be able to understand the

15
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:52,770
clinical presentation of each
one and the pathogenesis,

16
00:00:52,770 --> 00:00:55,630
so what's basically
happening during the course

17
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:59,990
of the infection, along
with being able to explain

18
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:03,690
the basic diagnosis and
treatment for each disease.

19
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:10,210
Okay so our first condition is

20
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:11,380
going to be rheumatic fever.

21
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:18,190
Now rheumatic fever is
considered a multi-system

22
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:20,170
disease which means it's
going to affect different

23
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:23,710
areas of your body and
that's going to happen

24
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:27,910
because of an autoimmune
-like reaction. So this is

25
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:32,850
technically a type 2
hypersensitivity reaction where

26
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:35,810
your immune system is
going to raise antibodies

27
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:39,350
against antigens on the
offending microbe, which

28
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:42,530
in this case is Streptococcus
pyogenes. And then

29
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:45,870
those antibodies have the
ability to then cross-react

30
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:49,030
with different cells and
tissues in your body.

31
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:53,250
Now, the effects of rheumatic
fever are not typically

32
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:56,750
seen during the primary
infection with strep,

33
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,250
but instead are going to
be seen a couple of weeks

34
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:02,630
later after that. And
this is generally going to

35
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:05,710
be associated with cases
of strep throat, so

36
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:09,430
pharyngitis, untreated upper
respiratory infections.

37
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:12,530
Now, there's going to
be a little bit of lag

38
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:15,430
time between the initial
infection and then

39
00:02:15,430 --> 00:02:18,590
any sort of rheumatic
fever symptoms because of

40
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:22,440
the time that it takes
to create antibodies.

41
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:27,190
All right, now the main
group of individuals that

42
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:29,030
are going to be affected
by this typically are going

43
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:32,070
to be children with an
average age of about 10.

44
00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:34,450
And this is certainly
going to be associated

45
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:38,690
with impoverished
areas of the world,

46
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:41,670
so different types of
countries that have or

47
00:02:41,670 --> 00:02:44,470
that don't have a
robust medical system.

48
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:48,600
If you consider the
incidence in affluent

49
00:02:48,790 --> 00:02:52,010
countries, it's going
to be relatively low due

50
00:02:52,010 --> 00:02:54,670
to the fact that cases
of strep throat can be

51
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:57,510
identified and treated
relatively easily. So

52
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:04,320
let's talk a little
bit about strep.

53
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:08,360
All right, so streptococcus
pyogenes that causes

54
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:12,280
rheumatic fever falls into
the group A streptococcus

55
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,900
landsfield grouping, which
is based on a surface

56
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,640
-level carbohydrate known
as the C-carbohydrate.

57
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:24,360
Now, some other aspects of
strep that you would expect

58
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:27,700
are based on, you know,
just the name of the genus.

59
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:31,800
So streptococcus, you
would expect to see coccus

60
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:36,700
-shaped circular cells that
are arranged in chains. And

61
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,500
so that's where streptococcus
comes from, in addition

62
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:43,140
to being gram-positive
right after a gram stain.

63
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:45,520
And you can see
that represented in

64
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:47,740
the microscopic
image on the slide.

65
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:51,400
Now, some of the
other points made here

66
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,860
are really ways that
you can differentiate

67
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:56,740
strep from other
different varieties of

68
00:03:56,740 --> 00:04:00,120
microbes, different
types of strep or staph.

69
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:05,120
And so the first note to
make, diagnostically speaking,

70
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:08,640
is that strep is going
to be catalase negative.

71
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:14,640
Now, catalase negative is
going to be a feature of

72
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:18,980
the streptococcus group in
addition to the enterococcus

73
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:21,720
group, which is
relatively related to the

74
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:24,580
streptococcus group, but
that's going to be different

75
00:04:24,580 --> 00:04:27,500
than staph, which is going
to be catalase positive.

76
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:33,100
Now, within the strep and
enterococcus group, you have

77
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:36,700
to have a way of diagnostically
separating those.

78
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:40,190
And one way that you can
do that is using hemolysis.

79
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:44,520
Now, strep pyogenes group A
tends to be beta hemolytic,

80
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:47,880
and that's brought about
by a virulence factor,

81
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:53,340
which is an exotoxin that
is secreted by the bacterial

82
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:56,900
cells known as streptolysin
S and streptolysin O.

83
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:01,400
So what happens is that
those toxins are capable

84
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:04,740
of degrading red blood
cells. And so if you grow

85
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:08,140
these bacteria on a blood
agar plate in a laboratory

86
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:11,020
the red blood cells will
be completely broken

87
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:15,360
down by the exotoxin and
the red color of the plate

88
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:19,560
will basically revert
back to a clear agar plate

89
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,740
now streptolysin s is
really the reason why

90
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:26,220
that happens because
that's an oxygen stable

91
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:32,300
exotoxin whereas streptolysin
o is not oxygen stable

92
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:35,420
streptolysin o is
important though because

93
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:40,440
Streptolysin O is immunogenic,
and the immune system

94
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:42,880
will raise antibodies
against that. So that's a

95
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:46,880
good diagnostic tool to be
able to identify whether

96
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,940
an individual had group
A strep infection or not.

97
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:56,040
Streptolysin S is
not immunogenic.

98
00:05:56,620 --> 00:06:00,900
Now, within the beta-hemolytic
group, you have to have

99
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:03,360
a way of differentiating
those, right? Because group

100
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:06,860
A, B, and C are all beta
hemolytic, so you have to have

101
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:09,600
a way of differentiating
those. And that's where other

102
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:13,560
different tests like the
susceptibility to bacitracin

103
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:17,750
come in. So group A strep
tends to be susceptible to

104
00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:22,200
bacitracin, whereas B and
C would be resistant to it.

105
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:23,880
You could also run
something like a

106
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:26,600
PYR test where group
A strep would be

107
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,560
positive and B and
C would be negative.

108
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:34,180
now the last point to make
here is about yet another

109
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:37,380
virulence factor which is
known as the m protein and

110
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:40,020
the m protein is really
important in rheumatic fever

111
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:43,580
because it is the antibodies
that are raised against

112
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:45,820
the m protein that are
going to be the cross

113
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,760
-reactive antibodies that
cause the issues in rheumatic

114
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:54,300
fever now from the perspective
of the bacterial cell

115
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:58,580
the m protein as a virulence
factor, is really just

116
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:02,740
a mechanism by which it is
going to try to evade the

117
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:06,180
immune system. So the M
protein is anti-phagocytic.

118
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:08,660
Now, in addition to that,

119
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:13,360
strep pyogenes will
secrete a capsule, and that

120
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:16,440
capsule happens to be
created from hyaluronic

121
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:20,500
acid. Now, because hyaluronic
acid is also naturally

122
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:24,420
found in human connective
tissues, the bacterium

123
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:28,280
basically has a capsule
that is inert in terms

124
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:32,640
of being identified by
the host immune system.

125
00:07:38,010 --> 00:07:40,410
Now it's that host
immune system that's

126
00:07:40,410 --> 00:07:43,050
actually responsible
for rheumatic fever.

127
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:46,850
So in the figure you could
see the basic process of

128
00:07:46,850 --> 00:07:51,110
group A strep infection
associated with the epithelium

129
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:54,690
in the throat and then the
activation of the immune

130
00:07:54,690 --> 00:07:58,870
system due to that infection
which is to be expected.

131
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:03,250
As a result, in rheumatic
fever, the immune system

132
00:08:03,250 --> 00:08:06,390
then is going to target
normal host proteins

133
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:10,770
like cardiac myosin in the
heart and laminin in the

134
00:08:10,770 --> 00:08:13,470
heart valves, and this
is going to be due to

135
00:08:13,470 --> 00:08:17,490
molecular mimicry. So
antibodies and T-cells that were

136
00:08:17,490 --> 00:08:20,210
initially formed against
the streptococcal M

137
00:08:20,210 --> 00:08:24,370
protein mistakenly cross
-react with the normal host

138
00:08:24,370 --> 00:08:28,050
proteins that happen to
be structurally similar,

139
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:31,430
and then that triggers
inflammation and damage.

140
00:08:36,990 --> 00:08:40,300
Now, there's no specific lab
test for rheumatic fever,

141
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:44,650
but there is instead the
Jones criteria as a system

142
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:49,070
for diagnosing acute rheumatic
fever. So first, what

143
00:08:49,070 --> 00:08:53,650
you need is evidence of a
preceding group A strep infection.

144
00:08:53,650 --> 00:08:57,390
And that could be done
with serological testing.

145
00:08:57,590 --> 00:09:02,770
So looking at antibody
titers for streptolysin O,

146
00:09:02,770 --> 00:09:07,730
or maybe DNase B, which
is another secreted enzyme

147
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:10,850
produced by strep that can
be used diagnostically.

148
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:13,730
Or even more simply,
just a positive

149
00:09:13,730 --> 00:09:16,250
throat culture or
a rapid strep test.

150
00:09:16,790 --> 00:09:20,310
So once the previous
infection is established,

151
00:09:20,310 --> 00:09:23,410
you could then use the
Jones criteria, which

152
00:09:23,410 --> 00:09:27,230
is a set of major criteria
to identify rheumatic

153
00:09:27,230 --> 00:09:30,190
fever, along with some
minor criteria that

154
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:34,250
you can see here. So
things like fever or a

155
00:09:34,250 --> 00:09:37,170
previous bout of rheumatic
fever, among others.

156
00:09:42,710 --> 00:09:47,190
As for the major criteria,
the term Jones is an

157
00:09:47,190 --> 00:09:50,450
acronym, most of which is
outlined here on the slide.

158
00:09:51,310 --> 00:09:56,070
so first j stands for
joints and that is going to

159
00:09:56,070 --> 00:09:59,810
be associated with the
polyarthritis that you see

160
00:09:59,810 --> 00:10:04,350
in rheumatic fever early
on so polyarthritis means

161
00:10:04,350 --> 00:10:06,370
that it's going to be
affecting multiple different

162
00:10:06,370 --> 00:10:10,550
joints and it's also
migratory so the location of

163
00:10:10,550 --> 00:10:13,970
the joints is going to
change over time it's also

164
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:17,450
generally associated with
the larger joints in the

165
00:10:17,450 --> 00:10:20,230
body and it's going to
affect them asymmetrically.

166
00:10:20,610 --> 00:10:24,750
So just because you have
a pain in a joint on the

167
00:10:24,750 --> 00:10:26,570
right hand side doesn't
mean that you necessarily

168
00:10:26,570 --> 00:10:29,650
have a pain in the
associated joint on the left.

169
00:10:30,490 --> 00:10:33,570
All right, I'm going
to skip over O for the

170
00:10:33,570 --> 00:10:36,010
moment here, and
that brings us to N,

171
00:10:36,010 --> 00:10:40,850
which is nodules. So
these are subcutaneous,

172
00:10:41,530 --> 00:10:46,430
small, painless nodules,
like you can see in the image

173
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:49,610
on the slide, that typically
are going to be seen on

174
00:10:49,610 --> 00:10:53,550
the elbows, sometimes also
on the hands or the feet.

175
00:10:54,030 --> 00:10:57,010
All right, E
stands for erythema

176
00:10:57,010 --> 00:11:00,910
marginatum, which is a
non-itchy rash, which

177
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:02,930
you could also see
here on the slide.

178
00:11:03,270 --> 00:11:06,930
And this is generally
going to be seen

179
00:11:06,930 --> 00:11:10,410
as these pink ring
-shaped lesions with

180
00:11:10,410 --> 00:11:14,130
pale centers and
slightly raised borders.

181
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:15,910
Usually they're going to be

182
00:11:15,910 --> 00:11:18,270
seen on the trunk
and the limbs.

183
00:11:18,690 --> 00:11:23,210
And lastly is
Seidenham's chorea, which

184
00:11:23,210 --> 00:11:25,510
is actually a
neurological disorder.

185
00:11:25,790 --> 00:11:31,400
That's going to be
characterized by uncontrollable

186
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:37,490
spasmodic movements, typically
of the head and the face,

187
00:11:37,490 --> 00:11:42,470
sometimes the tongue as
well, and the limbs also.

188
00:11:43,050 --> 00:11:44,170
Interestingly,

189
00:11:44,310 --> 00:11:46,550
this tends to manifest in

190
00:11:46,550 --> 00:11:48,610
females more than
it does males.

191
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:58,240
Okay, so the letter
I left out was O,

192
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:01,320
which you could think
more about like the

193
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:05,020
shape of a heart and which
is also the most life

194
00:12:05,020 --> 00:12:07,380
-threatening component
of rheumatic fever.

195
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:11,300
Now, the condition can
lead to pancarditis, which

196
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:15,060
takes into account all the
layers of the heart and it

197
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:19,950
can lead to endocarditis
myocarditis or pericarditis

198
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:24,760
so like endocarditis
rheumatic fever will

199
00:12:24,760 --> 00:12:27,380
often have an
associated heart murmur

200
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:31,280
due to vegetations on
the heart valves and

201
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:33,960
this will often affect
the left side of the

202
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:37,850
heart most commonly the
mitral valve pericarditis

203
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:41,700
can be associated
with pain in the chest

204
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:45,300
And myocardial
inflammation can affect the

205
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:49,140
electrical conduction,
leading to a disrupted ECG.

206
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:52,560
From a histological standpoint,

207
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:56,140
the inflamed myocardium
will also display

208
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:59,600
structures known as
Ashoff bodies, which

209
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:01,760
are granulomas in
the connective tissue

210
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:04,520
of the heart due to
chronic inflammation.

211
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:08,200
Now, those contain
Anishkau cells, which

212
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:11,480
are abnormal
macrophages, and you can

213
00:13:11,480 --> 00:13:14,560
see both of those
images on the slide.

214
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:17,380
Now, it's structures like
these that can lead to

215
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:22,840
scarring, and if there's a
repeated exposure to group A

216
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:26,460
strep infections, rheumatic
fever can ultimately become

217
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:30,220
chronic, leading to more
scarring and the potential

218
00:13:30,220 --> 00:13:35,100
for valve stenosis or even
congestive heart failure.

219
00:13:35,700 --> 00:13:39,460
Now, in terms of treating
acute rheumatic fever,

220
00:13:39,480 --> 00:13:42,840
even though it's an immune
-mediated condition, you

221
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:45,500
would want to use antibiotics
to be sure that there's

222
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:47,480
no bacteria that remain
that could cause an

223
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:51,160
infection, in addition to
using anti-inflammatories.

224
00:13:55,270 --> 00:13:58,820
okay so we're going to
move on to bacterial

225
00:13:58,820 --> 00:14:02,080
vasculitis specifically
Rocky Mountain spotted

226
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:11,480
fever the condition is
caused by a species of

227
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:14,980
bacteria known as
Rickettsia Rickettsii Now,

228
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:17,980
rickettsial bacteria are
kind of unique because

229
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:21,180
they are obligate
intracellular pathogens.

230
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:26,000
They can't exist on their
own outside of other cells.

231
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,740
And the reason for that
is because they lack

232
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:31,660
some of the genes to
make their own energy.

233
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:34,040
So really, they're
just basically

234
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:36,240
energy parasites
of other cells.

235
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:40,340
Now, in terms of the
bacterial profile,

236
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:44,580
rickettsial bacteria
technically have a gram-negative

237
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:48,920
type wall, but they are very
difficult to gram stain.

238
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:51,000
And so in order
to visualize them,

239
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:53,500
typically other staining
procedures are going

240
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:55,600
to be utilized,
like Gimsa staining.

241
00:14:56,690 --> 00:15:01,320
All right, now, in order
to contract rickettsia, you

242
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:05,160
need that bacterium to be
vectored. And so when thinking

243
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:08,140
about Rocky Mountain spotted
fever, it's really just

244
00:15:08,140 --> 00:15:10,920
as important to talk about
the vector as much as it

245
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:14,220
is to talk about the causative
agent, the bacterium.

246
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:19,700
So the vector for the
disease is going to be the

247
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:22,600
dog tick, which is known
as Dermocenter variabilis.

248
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:26,480
And the distribution
of the tick is

249
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:29,280
basically going to
be everywhere in the

250
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:31,760
United States east of
the Rocky Mountains.

251
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:35,980
So the naming convention of
the disease is a little bit

252
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:39,600
of a misnomer. So it really
is not associated with

253
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:42,080
the West or with the Rocky
Mountains. It's going to be

254
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:45,840
much more endemic, seen
much more frequently in the

255
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:49,280
eastern United States, specifically
the southeastern United

256
00:15:49,280 --> 00:15:53,320
States. And you can even
see on the map on the slide,

257
00:15:53,320 --> 00:15:57,420
basically there's a belt
between North Carolina and

258
00:15:57,420 --> 00:16:00,080
Oklahoma where most of the
cases are going to be seen.

259
00:16:00,590 --> 00:16:02,240
Now, that's not to
say that you can't

260
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:04,370
see it sporadically
in the west also.

261
00:16:05,150 --> 00:16:07,060
In that case, it's
not going to be the

262
00:16:07,060 --> 00:16:08,760
dog tick that's going
to be the vector.

263
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:11,220
There's another
species of dermacenter

264
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:14,380
known as dermacenter
andersoni that

265
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:16,800
will be the vector
in that location.

266
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:24,380
The reservoir for the bacteria
is primarily the infected

267
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:28,940
ticks. So they harbor the
bacteria and they transmit

268
00:16:28,940 --> 00:16:33,360
the bacteria. So humans
and other animals are kind

269
00:16:33,360 --> 00:16:36,240
of basically accidental
hosts because they're really

270
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:39,000
not required for the
completion of the life cycle.

271
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:46,800
But the animals can
act as reservoirs,

272
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:50,300
right? So rodents or
rabbits can act as

273
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:52,560
reservoirs or maybe
as amplifying hosts.

274
00:16:52,920 --> 00:16:56,760
But the ticks really are
the permanent natural

275
00:16:56,760 --> 00:17:00,080
reservoir. are. So they
carry the bacteria, and they

276
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:04,880
actually pass them to their
offspring through a process

277
00:17:04,880 --> 00:17:08,040
known as transovarial
transmission. So basically, a

278
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:10,340
female tick that's infected
with rickettsial bacteria

279
00:17:10,340 --> 00:17:15,040
can transmit the bacteria
directly to her eggs, and

280
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:17,560
so the larvae, as they
hatch, are going to be born

281
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:20,460
with the bacteria. So you
don't really necessarily

282
00:17:20,460 --> 00:17:24,440
need a secondary host like
a human or another animal.

283
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:29,120
All right, now if a human
does contract the bacteria

284
00:17:29,340 --> 00:17:31,880
following a tick bite,
the bacteria is going to

285
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:35,360
enter into the body and then
spread in the bloodstream.

286
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:39,870
So there's bacteremia
associated with this, and

287
00:17:39,870 --> 00:17:42,400
usually that's going to be
in the window of about 7

288
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:45,020
to 10 days before the
symptoms are going to start.

289
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:49,100
At that point, the bacteria
that are going to attach

290
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:52,640
and invade the vascular
endothelial cells, right,

291
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:54,800
the lining of the blood
vessels throughout the body.

292
00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:58,760
And it's really that
widespread invasion

293
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:01,250
that's going to lead
to the vasculitis.

294
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:09,000
So what do we expect
to see in a patient

295
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,520
that has Rocky
Mountain Spotted Fever?

296
00:18:11,860 --> 00:18:16,920
So early on in the infection,
it's going to appear like

297
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:20,960
a patient just has some sort
of viral infection maybe

298
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:25,680
a flu it's going to just show
you know basic nonspecific

299
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:29,360
symptoms something like
a fever you know general

300
00:18:29,360 --> 00:18:33,880
pain vomiting or a headache
now the headache could be

301
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:36,720
a good indicator so typically
the headache is going

302
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:40,600
to be severe and that's going
to be due to the vasculitis

303
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:44,080
of the vessels in the brain
leading to that prominent

304
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:46,980
headache so it's certainly
something to consider.

305
00:18:47,290 --> 00:18:50,180
Now within a couple of
days you would see then

306
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:53,280
the characteristic rash
associated with the

307
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:56,720
condition which is going
to stem from the vasculitis

308
00:18:56,720 --> 00:18:59,040
and the hemorrhaging
of the of the vessels.

309
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:01,980
Now that rash is typically
going to start on

310
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:04,480
the wrists and the ankles
and then move up the

311
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:07,880
extremities to the to
the trunk of the body.

312
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:12,840
Now due to the vasculitis
and the inflammation

313
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:16,480
you know, the leakage of
the blood vessels, the

314
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:19,340
hemorrhaging, the clotting,
and the effect on the

315
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:23,080
blood flow then has the
ability to affect critical

316
00:19:23,080 --> 00:19:27,260
organs in the body.
So there can be damage

317
00:19:27,260 --> 00:19:31,400
to the brain, the lungs,
or the heart. So Rocky

318
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:34,840
Mountain spotted fever
can rapidly lead to fatal

319
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:39,020
complications if left
undiagnosed or unchecked.

320
00:19:43,870 --> 00:19:47,270
In terms of diagnosis,
it's usually going

321
00:19:47,270 --> 00:19:50,710
to be based on clinical
symptoms as well as

322
00:19:50,710 --> 00:19:54,230
a history of potential
tick exposure rather

323
00:19:54,230 --> 00:19:56,870
than waiting on
laboratory test results.

324
00:19:57,070 --> 00:20:00,710
Now some tests that you
can conduct would be

325
00:20:00,710 --> 00:20:03,970
serological testing to
identify antibodies in the

326
00:20:03,970 --> 00:20:08,170
patient or trying to identify
the rickettsial bacteria

327
00:20:08,170 --> 00:20:12,330
either by way of a skin
biopsy or a PCR method.

328
00:20:13,430 --> 00:20:18,370
Because the infection progresses
rapidly and can become

329
00:20:18,370 --> 00:20:22,350
fatal, typically treatment
with an antibiotic like

330
00:20:22,350 --> 00:20:26,210
doxycycline is usually
going to be started almost

331
00:20:26,210 --> 00:20:30,030
immediately if Rocky Mountain
spotted fever is suspected.

332
00:20:33,890 --> 00:20:36,650
All right, so the last
condition we're going

333
00:20:36,650 --> 00:20:39,230
to discuss here is going
to be Chagas disease.

334
00:20:39,230 --> 00:20:42,520
otherwise known as
American trypanosomiasis.

335
00:20:42,570 --> 00:20:46,170
And I want to emphasize
American just to

336
00:20:46,170 --> 00:20:49,110
differentiate this from
the African trypanosomes.

337
00:20:54,150 --> 00:20:57,850
All right, so Chagas
disease is caused by a

338
00:20:57,850 --> 00:21:01,090
protozoal parasite known
as trypanosoma cruzi.

339
00:21:01,490 --> 00:21:06,690
And trypanosomes, along with
other related organisms,

340
00:21:06,690 --> 00:21:10,190
have the ability to change
morphological form throughout

341
00:21:10,190 --> 00:21:14,110
their life cycle. Now,
in terms of trypanosoma

342
00:21:14,110 --> 00:21:16,870
cruzi, there are two
morphological forms associated

343
00:21:16,870 --> 00:21:21,650
with it, the trypamastigote
and the amastigote form. So

344
00:21:21,650 --> 00:21:24,830
the trypamastigote form is
going to be the infective

345
00:21:24,830 --> 00:21:29,510
form that you're going to
find in the human blood very

346
00:21:29,510 --> 00:21:32,090
early on in the infection,
and that's highlighted

347
00:21:32,090 --> 00:21:35,950
in the first image on the
slide with the black arrow.

348
00:21:36,520 --> 00:21:38,250
Now there are some structural

349
00:21:38,250 --> 00:21:40,590
features to the
trypamastigote form.

350
00:21:40,850 --> 00:21:46,770
First is the undulating
membrane. So a trypimastogote

351
00:21:46,770 --> 00:21:49,390
form of trypanosoma cruzi
will typically take on

352
00:21:49,390 --> 00:21:54,810
this kind of C-shaped
format, and the undulating

353
00:21:54,810 --> 00:21:59,530
membrane can be seen on
the top of the parasite. So

354
00:21:59,530 --> 00:22:02,630
this is a flagellum that
runs along the length of one

355
00:22:02,630 --> 00:22:05,830
side of the parasite and
is housed within the plasma

356
00:22:05,830 --> 00:22:08,850
membrane, making this kind
of sail-like structure,

357
00:22:08,850 --> 00:22:11,440
And that's going to
terminate in a flagellum,

358
00:22:11,490 --> 00:22:15,170
which you can see down at
the bottom of the parasite.

359
00:22:16,510 --> 00:22:21,090
Now, the trypamastigote
form has the ability to then

360
00:22:21,090 --> 00:22:25,150
identify, target, and
invade different cells and

361
00:22:25,150 --> 00:22:28,330
tissues within the body, at
which point it's going to

362
00:22:28,330 --> 00:22:30,950
change morphological form
into what's known as the

363
00:22:30,950 --> 00:22:34,690
amastigote form. And the
amastigote form is going to be

364
00:22:34,690 --> 00:22:37,870
a form that is capable of
dividing very frequently.

365
00:22:38,130 --> 00:22:41,170
So this is an intracellular
form, and it doesn't

366
00:22:41,170 --> 00:22:44,270
have any of the same
structural features as

367
00:22:44,270 --> 00:22:47,080
the tripomastigote form
would have. So there

368
00:22:47,370 --> 00:22:50,690
is no flagellum. There's
no undulating membrane.

369
00:22:50,690 --> 00:22:54,490
It's a much smaller
cell, much more reduced.

370
00:22:54,490 --> 00:22:58,870
And as it is housed within
the cell, it's going

371
00:22:58,870 --> 00:23:02,650
to undergo division,
creating a group of cells

372
00:23:02,650 --> 00:23:06,990
within that invaded cell.
And it's going to create

373
00:23:06,990 --> 00:23:09,690
basically this structure
that looks like a nest

374
00:23:09,690 --> 00:23:15,110
or a clump of amastogote
cells known as a pseudocyst.

375
00:23:15,110 --> 00:23:18,310
It's not really a
true cyst because the

376
00:23:19,850 --> 00:23:23,330
parasite is not actually
secreting any sort of cyst

377
00:23:23,330 --> 00:23:26,950
wall. It's just housed
within the infected cell.

378
00:23:30,470 --> 00:23:33,930
all right so we're talking
about another condition here

379
00:23:33,930 --> 00:23:37,730
that requires a vector
and in the case of chagas

380
00:23:37,730 --> 00:23:41,350
disease the vector is known
as the reduvid bug otherwise

381
00:23:41,350 --> 00:23:45,590
known as the kissing or the
assassin bug now cases of

382
00:23:45,590 --> 00:23:48,770
chagas disease then are
going to be correlated to the

383
00:23:48,770 --> 00:23:52,110
distribution of the vector
So typically, you're going

384
00:23:52,110 --> 00:23:56,770
to see cases in Central
and South America, although

385
00:23:56,810 --> 00:24:00,970
raduvid bugs have been
identified as far north as Texas.

386
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:04,390
Now, the bug tends
to bite at nighttime,

387
00:24:04,390 --> 00:24:07,370
and it will also
tend to bite around

388
00:24:07,370 --> 00:24:09,850
the head or the face
of an individual.

389
00:24:10,010 --> 00:24:12,630
That's also why it got
its name, the kissing bug.

390
00:24:13,170 --> 00:24:16,350
Now, at the time that
it's taking a blood meal,

391
00:24:16,350 --> 00:24:20,210
it will also defecate,
and in the feces, you will

392
00:24:20,210 --> 00:24:24,070
have the parasite in
trypomastigote form. So now

393
00:24:24,070 --> 00:24:27,330
the parasite has an entry
point into the body.

394
00:24:27,530 --> 00:24:30,330
Now, once the
parasite gets into

395
00:24:30,330 --> 00:24:31,890
the bloodstream
and the lymphatics,

396
00:24:32,030 --> 00:24:34,410
it could basically go anywhere.

397
00:24:34,630 --> 00:24:39,490
And so even though it
can basically invade any

398
00:24:39,490 --> 00:24:43,070
nucleated cell, it tends
to have a stronger affinity

399
00:24:43,070 --> 00:24:46,750
for specific tissues in
the body. So things like

400
00:24:46,750 --> 00:24:51,230
muscle tissue, the liver,
the spleen, the central

401
00:24:51,230 --> 00:24:55,030
or peripheral nervous
system, among others. And

402
00:24:55,030 --> 00:24:58,770
once it invades a tissue,
it will again convert

403
00:24:58,770 --> 00:25:02,850
to the amastigote form.
It will begin to undergo

404
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:06,490
division and create
pseudocysts within the tissue.

405
00:25:10,550 --> 00:25:14,470
So wherever the bug bites
and the parasite enters

406
00:25:14,470 --> 00:25:17,090
into the body there's
going to be a localized

407
00:25:17,090 --> 00:25:20,790
reaction that's referred
to as a shagoma and

408
00:25:20,790 --> 00:25:23,170
you can see that down on
the bottom of the slide

409
00:25:23,170 --> 00:25:26,250
with the picture highlighted
by the black arrow

410
00:25:27,450 --> 00:25:31,030
now i already said that
the bug likes to bite

411
00:25:31,030 --> 00:25:34,510
around the head and
the face and oftentimes

412
00:25:34,630 --> 00:25:38,010
the bite is going to be
close enough to the eye where

413
00:25:38,010 --> 00:25:40,330
the parasite could either
be rubbed into the eye

414
00:25:40,330 --> 00:25:44,350
or can just gain access
by way of the conjunctiva.

415
00:25:44,510 --> 00:25:47,350
And if that's the case,
the swelling is going

416
00:25:47,350 --> 00:25:49,810
to be around the eye,
and that's referred

417
00:25:49,810 --> 00:25:52,250
to as Romagna's sign.
And you can see that

418
00:25:52,250 --> 00:25:54,730
highlighted in the picture
with the red arrows.

419
00:25:55,810 --> 00:25:58,890
Now, both of these are going
to be hallmark physical

420
00:25:58,890 --> 00:26:02,010
indicators of the acute
phase of Chagas disease.

421
00:26:02,730 --> 00:26:06,610
Over the next few weeks,
an individual can either

422
00:26:06,610 --> 00:26:10,970
be asymptomatic or could
have just some general

423
00:26:10,970 --> 00:26:14,170
mild symptoms, you
know, things like fever,

424
00:26:14,370 --> 00:26:17,530
body aches, fatigue,
along with potential

425
00:26:17,530 --> 00:26:22,150
swelling of the spleen or
the liver. And ultimately,

426
00:26:22,150 --> 00:26:25,210
those symptoms can
resolve on their own.

427
00:26:29,070 --> 00:26:32,090
Now, most people are going
to enter into a lifelong

428
00:26:32,090 --> 00:26:35,750
asymptomatic stage where
the parasites are basically

429
00:26:35,750 --> 00:26:39,510
hidden and don't really
cause any issues but some

430
00:26:39,510 --> 00:26:41,810
other people are going to
progress to a symptomatic

431
00:26:41,810 --> 00:26:46,090
stage anywhere from years
to decades later and can

432
00:26:46,090 --> 00:26:48,790
display some pretty
significant complications

433
00:26:48,790 --> 00:26:53,310
because of the chronic
infection like heart issues that

434
00:26:53,310 --> 00:26:57,710
include inflammation
arrhythmias or just enlargement

435
00:26:57,710 --> 00:27:00,610
of the heart now other
complications that could

436
00:27:00,610 --> 00:27:03,410
be seen in the chronic form
of the disease are known

437
00:27:03,410 --> 00:27:07,750
as megaesophagus and
megacolon. And that's going

438
00:27:07,750 --> 00:27:11,100
to be due to the parasite
targeting the autonomic

439
00:27:11,100 --> 00:27:14,730
nerves and the smooth
muscle of the GI tract over

440
00:27:14,730 --> 00:27:18,170
time. And this causes the
characteristic enlargement,

441
00:27:18,510 --> 00:27:22,090
the loss of the muscle
tone, and will disrupt

442
00:27:22,090 --> 00:27:24,830
things like peristalsis
and has the potential

443
00:27:24,830 --> 00:27:27,610
to even inhibit the
act of swallowing.

444
00:27:33,260 --> 00:27:36,540
So diagnosing Chagas disease
really depends on the

445
00:27:36,540 --> 00:27:40,580
stage of the infection. In
the acute stage, you could

446
00:27:40,580 --> 00:27:44,500
look for parasites in a blood
smear or a tissue biopsy,

447
00:27:44,500 --> 00:27:48,460
or you could use a technique
like PCR to detect it.

448
00:27:48,580 --> 00:27:51,500
In chronic Chagas disease,

449
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:54,700
the parasite levels in
the blood drop pretty

450
00:27:54,700 --> 00:27:58,300
significantly, and so that
makes things like microscopy

451
00:27:58,300 --> 00:28:01,680
a little less effective.
So at that point, diagnosis

452
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:04,700
is really going to rely
on serological testing.

453
00:28:05,240 --> 00:28:07,440
Now in the acute
phase, you can also

454
00:28:07,440 --> 00:28:10,020
use antiparasitic
medications to clear

455
00:28:10,020 --> 00:28:11,780
the organism from
the bloodstream.

456
00:28:12,200 --> 00:28:15,760
But in the chronic form,
medications unfortunately

457
00:28:15,760 --> 00:28:18,380
can't really reverse
any type of tissue or

458
00:28:18,380 --> 00:28:20,900
organ damage that might
have already occurred. So

459
00:28:40,470 --> 00:28:42,790
that concludes the
lecture. I just wanted

460
00:28:42,790 --> 00:28:44,910
to thank everyone
for listening, and I

461
00:28:44,910 --> 00:28:47,130
wanted to provide the
link for feedback.